ner first, reflecting the chaos and competition of the moment.","LocalizedString":"Victory banners became powerful symbols for the Red Army across the Eastern Front. Units often raised red flags to celebrate major gains, from city centers to enemy fortifications. These banners, whether handmade or official, served to signal triumph and boost morale. By the final year of the war, they had become essential emblems of Soviet success. As the Red Army closed in on Berlin in April 1945, Soviet command prepared several Victory Banners to ensure a flag would be raised over the German Reichstag. The political significance of this act was so great that multiple flags were distributed to different assault groups, each tasked with storming the building. Soviet leadership feared that heavy resistance or chance could prevent one unit from reaching the rooftop. To guarantee the symbolic moment occurred, at least nine banners were made and assigned to front-line units. These flags were carried into intense combat as soldiers fought room by room through the Reichstag. The 150th Rifle Division, led by General Vasily Shatilov, received one such banner, which would later be recognized as the official Victory Banner. The team that ultimately raised the banner included Sergeant Mikhail Yegorov and Sergeant Meliton Kantaria. Their flag was hoisted on May 1, 1945, though the act was later restaged for photographs. Other units also claimed to have raised that banner first, reflecting the chaos and competition of the moment."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Convert"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Mother_Russia'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/Mother_Russia.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/NavalWarfare/t_victory_banners.t_victory_banners","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Soviet WWII Poster","historyPhoto"